  "atoms": [
    {
      "id": "atom-1",
      "text": "Over the past 40 years, the Uyghur population in Xinjiang, in China, grew from 5.55 million to 12 million.",
      "sourceText": "Over the past 40 years, the Uyghur population in Xinjiang, in China, grew from 5.55 million to 12 million.",
      "start": 0,
      "end": 106,
      "role": "NUMERIC_CONSTRAINT"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "atomId": "atom-1",
      "spans": [
        {
          "id": "doc-8c62afe872e1::sentence-4",
          "documentId": "doc-8c62afe872e1",
          "text": "Evidence 1: Are there reliable population figures for the Uyghur population in Xinjiang, in China over the last 40 years? ",
          "start": 199,
          "end": 321,
          "context": "Source URL: https://china.usc.edu/whats-stake-xinjiang\n Evidence 1: Are there reliable population figures for the Uyghur population in Xinjiang, in China over the last 40 years?  No. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-22d3f92a3016::sentence-4",
          "documentId": "doc-22d3f92a3016",
          "text": "Evidence 1: what is the birthrate of the Uyghur population in Xinjiang Birth rates in the mostly Uighur regions of Hotan and Kashgar plunged by more than 60% from 2015 to 2018, the latest year available in government statistics. ",
          "start": 216,
          "end": 445,
          "context": "Source URL: https://apnews.com/article/269b3de1af34e17c1941a514f78d764c\n Evidence 1: what is the birthrate of the Uyghur population in Xinjiang Birth rates in the mostly Uighur regions of Hotan and Kashgar plunged by more than 60% from 2015 to 2018, the latest year available in government statistics.  Across the Xinjiang region, birth rates continue to plummet, falling nearly 24% last year alone — compared to just 4.2% nationwide, statistics show.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-22d3f92a3016::sentence-33",
          "documentId": "doc-22d3f92a3016",
          "text": "The hundreds of millions of dollars the government pours into birth control has transformed Xinjiang from one of China’s fastest-growing regions to among its slowest in just a few years, according to new research obtained by The Associated Press in advance of publication by China scholar Adrian Zenz.\n",
          "start": 4657,
          "end": 4959,
          "context": "Across the Xinjiang region, birth rates continue to plummet, falling nearly 24% last year alone — compared to just 4.2% nationwide, statistics show.\n The hundreds of millions of dollars the government pours into birth control has transformed Xinjiang from one of China’s fastest-growing regions to among its slowest in just a few years, according to new research obtained by The Associated Press in advance of publication by China scholar Adrian Zenz.\n “This kind of drop is unprecedented....there’s a ruthlessness to it,” said Zenz, a leading expert in the policing of China’s minority regions. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-22d3f92a3016::sentence-8",
          "documentId": "doc-22d3f92a3016",
          "text": "The campaign over the past four years in the far west region of Xinjiang is leading to what some experts are calling a form of “demographic genocide.”\n\n",
          "start": 1209,
          "end": 1361,
          "context": "While individual women have spoken out before about forced birth control, the practice is far more widespread and systematic than previously known, according to an AP investigation based on government statistics, state documents and interviews with 30 ex-detainees, family members and a former detention camp instructor.  The campaign over the past four years in the far west region of Xinjiang is leading to what some experts are calling a form of “demographic genocide.”\n\n The state regularly subjects minority women to pregnancy checks, and forces intrauterine devices, sterilization and even abortion on hundreds of thousands, the interviews and data show. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-22d3f92a3016::sentence-18",
          "documentId": "doc-22d3f92a3016",
          "text": "The campaign over the past four years in the far west region of Xinjiang is leading to what some experts are calling a form of “demographic genocide.”\n",
          "start": 2690,
          "end": 2841,
          "context": "While individual women have spoken out before about forced birth control, the practice is far more widespread and systematic than previously known, according to an AP investigation based on government statistics, state documents and interviews with 30 ex-detainees, family members and a former detention camp instructor.  The campaign over the past four years in the far west region of Xinjiang is leading to what some experts are calling a form of “demographic genocide.”\n The state regularly subjects minority women to pregnancy checks, and forces intrauterine devices, sterilization and even abortion on hundreds of thousands, the interviews and data show. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-22d3f92a3016::sentence-61",
          "documentId": "doc-22d3f92a3016",
          "text": "After the People’s Liberation Army swept through in 1949, China’s new Communist rulers ordered thousands of soldiers to settle in Xinjiang, pushing the Han population from 6.7% that year to more than 40% by 1980. ",
          "start": 8843,
          "end": 9056,
          "context": "For centuries, the majority was Muslim in the arid, landlocked region China now calls “Xinjiang” — meaning “New Frontier” in Mandarin.\n After the People’s Liberation Army swept through in 1949, China’s new Communist rulers ordered thousands of soldiers to settle in Xinjiang, pushing the Han population from 6.7% that year to more than 40% by 1980.  The move sowed anxiety about Chinese migration that persists to this day. "
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
